2026-04-27 14:13:52 | ERROR    | __main__:validation_exception_handler:87 - 422 Validation Error at /api/v1/tts: [{'type': 'string_too_long', 'loc': ('body', 'text'), 'msg': 'String should have at most 5000 characters', 'input': 'Definition / Introduction\n\nCell pores, also known as transmembrane channels or porins, are small openings or channels in the cell membrane that allow the passage of molecules, ions, or water across the cell membrane. They play a crucial role in maintaining cellular homeostasis, regulating the movement of substances in and out of the cell, and facilitating various cellular processes.\n\nCore Concept Explanation\n\nCell pores can be broadly classified into two categories:\n\n- Selective pores: These pores are highly specific and allow the passage of only certain molecules or ions, while restricting the movement of others. Examples include aquaporins, which facilitate water transport across the cell membrane.\n- Non-selective pores: These pores allow the passage of a wide range of molecules or ions, without any specificity. Examples include ion channels, which regulate the movement of ions across the cell membrane.\n\nMechanism / Process (step-wise)\n\nThe process of cell pore-mediated transport involves the following steps:\n\n1. Binding of molecules: Molecules or ions bind to the pore, either through electrostatic interactions or hydrophobic interactions.\n2. Conformational change: The pore undergoes a conformational change, allowing the bound molecule to pass through the pore.\n3. Transport across the membrane: The molecule or ion is transported across the cell membrane, either through the pore or through a nearby channel.\n4. Release of molecules: The molecule or ion is released on the other side of the membrane, either into the cytosol or into the extracellular space.\n\nKey Components / Factors\n\n- Pore structure: The structure of the pore, including its size, shape, and composition, determines its selectivity and transport properties.\n- Pore density: The density of pores in the cell membrane affects the rate of transport across the membrane.\n- Pore regulation: Pores can be regulated by various mechanisms, including phosphorylation, ubiquitination, and binding of regulatory proteins.\n\nApplications / Importance\n\n- Cellular homeostasis: Cell pores play a crucial role in maintaining cellular homeostasis by regulating the movement of substances in and out of the cell.\n- Disease mechanisms: Abnormalities in cell pore function have been implicated in various diseases, including cancer, diabetes, and neurodegenerative disorders.\n- Drug delivery: Cell pores can be targeted for drug delivery, allowing for the selective uptake of therapeutic molecules by cells.\n\nLimitations\n\n- Selectivity: Cell pores can be non-selective, allowing the passage of unwanted molecules or ions.\n- Regulation: Pores can be difficult to regulate, leading to uncontrolled transport across the membrane.\n- Toxicity: Abnormalities in pore function can lead to cellular toxicity and disease.\n\nConclusion / Summary\n\nCell pores are essential components of the cell membrane, regulating the movement of substances in and out of the cell. Understanding the structure, function, and regulation of cell pores is crucial for understanding various cellular processes and disease mechanisms.\n\nFlowchart / Diagram Representation (Textual)\n\n```\n                                      +---------------+\n                                        Cell Membrane  \n                                      +---------------+\n                                             \n                                             \n                                             v\n                                      +---------------+\n                                        Pore Structure  \n                                        (size, shape,    \n                                         composition)    \n                                      +---------------+\n                                             \n                                             \n                                             v\n                                      +---------------+\n                                        Pore Density    \n                                        (number of pores)\n                                      +---------------+\n                                             \n                                             \n                                             v\n                                      +---------------+\n                                        Pore Regulation  \n                                        (phosphorylation,  \n                                         ubiquitination,  \n                                         binding of regulatory\n                                         proteins)        \n                                      +---------------+\n                                             \n                                             \n                                             v\n                                      +---------------+\n                                        Transport Across  \n                                        the Membrane     \n                                      +---------------+\n                                             \n                                             \n                                             v\n                                      +---------------+\n                                        Release of Molecules\n                                        (into cytosol or  \n                                         extracellular space)\n                                      +---------------+\n```', 'ctx': {'max_length': 5000}}]
2026-04-27 14:13:52 | ERROR    | __main__:validation_exception_handler:88 - Body: b'{"text":"Definition / Introduction\\n\\nCell pores, also known as transmembrane channels or porins, are small openings or channels in the cell membrane that allow the passage of molecules, ions, or water across the cell membrane. They play a crucial role in maintaining cellular homeostasis, regulating the movement of substances in and out of the cell, and facilitating various cellular processes.\\n\\nCore Concept Explanation\\n\\nCell pores can be broadly classified into two categories:\\n\\n- Selective pores: These pores are highly specific and allow the passage of only certain molecules or ions, while restricting the movement of others. Examples include aquaporins, which facilitate water transport across the cell membrane.\\n- Non-selective pores: These pores allow the passage of a wide range of molecules or ions, without any specificity. Examples include ion channels, which regulate the movement of ions across the cell membrane.\\n\\nMechanism / Process (step-wise)\\n\\nThe process of cell pore-mediated transport involves the following steps:\\n\\n1. Binding of molecules: Molecules or ions bind to the pore, either through electrostatic interactions or hydrophobic interactions.\\n2. Conformational change: The pore undergoes a conformational change, allowing the bound molecule to pass through the pore.\\n3. Transport across the membrane: The molecule or ion is transported across the cell membrane, either through the pore or through a nearby channel.\\n4. Release of molecules: The molecule or ion is released on the other side of the membrane, either into the cytosol or into the extracellular space.\\n\\nKey Components / Factors\\n\\n- Pore structure: The structure of the pore, including its size, shape, and composition, determines its selectivity and transport properties.\\n- Pore density: The density of pores in the cell membrane affects the rate of transport across the membrane.\\n- Pore regulation: Pores can be regulated by various mechanisms, including phosphorylation, ubiquitination, and binding of regulatory proteins.\\n\\nApplications / Importance\\n\\n- Cellular homeostasis: Cell pores play a crucial role in maintaining cellular homeostasis by regulating the movement of substances in and out of the cell.\\n- Disease mechanisms: Abnormalities in cell pore function have been implicated in various diseases, including cancer, diabetes, and neurodegenerative disorders.\\n- Drug delivery: Cell pores can be targeted for drug delivery, allowing for the selective uptake of therapeutic molecules by cells.\\n\\nLimitations\\n\\n- Selectivity: Cell pores can be non-selective, allowing the passage of unwanted molecules or ions.\\n- Regulation: Pores can be difficult to regulate, leading to uncontrolled transport across the membrane.\\n- Toxicity: Abnormalities in pore function can lead to cellular toxicity and disease.\\n\\nConclusion / Summary\\n\\nCell pores are essential components of the cell membrane, regulating the movement of substances in and out of the cell. Understanding the structure, function, and regulation of cell pores is crucial for understanding various cellular processes and disease mechanisms.\\n\\nFlowchart / Diagram Representation (Textual)\\n\\n```\\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                        Cell Membrane  \\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                             \\n                                             \\n                                             v\\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                        Pore Structure  \\n                                        (size, shape,    \\n                                         composition)    \\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                             \\n                                             \\n                                             v\\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                        Pore Density    \\n                                        (number of pores)\\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                             \\n                                             \\n                                             v\\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                        Pore Regulation  \\n                                        (phosphorylation,  \\n                                         ubiquitination,  \\n                                         binding of regulatory\\n                                         proteins)        \\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                             \\n                                             \\n                                             v\\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                        Transport Across  \\n                                        the Membrane     \\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                             \\n                                             \\n                                             v\\n                                      +---------------+\\n                                        Release of Molecules\\n                                        (into cytosol or  \\n                                         extracellular space)\\n                                      +---------------+\\n```","voice_style":"professor","mode":"Expert","speed":1.0}'
